WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.820
The Hierarchical File System contains the following types of files.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.820
There's directories, regular files, named pipes or FIFO,

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.820
character special files and symbolic links.

4
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.820
Users and programmers create directories,

5
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.820
regular files, pipes and symbolic links

6
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.820
and the system programmer creates character special files.

7
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.820
Let's take a look at these in turn.

8
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.820
A directory is just a file just like everything else

9
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.820
but it's a special kind of file.

10
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.820
It consists of the name of a set of files

11
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.820
and other information about them.

12
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.820
Usually the files in a directory are related to each other in some way

13
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.820
and of course a directory can have subdirectories.

14
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.820
A zos-unix user has a directory defined as its home directory.

15
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.820
After logon, the home directory becomes the working directory.

16
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.820
A regular file is a named unit of text or binary data information.

17
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.820
A file can be, for example, a piece of C source code,

18
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.820
a list of names or places, a printer formatted document,

19
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.820
a string of numbers organized in a certain way,

20
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.820
an employee record or a memo.

21
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.820
A user or application must understand how to access

22
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.820
and use the individual increments of data within a file.

23
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.820
When the sticky bit is set on for a directory,

24
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.820
only the super user or the owner can delete

25
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.820
or remove a file in the directory.

26
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.820
When the sticky bit is set on for an executable program,

27
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.820
zos-unix looks for that program in the user's step lib,

28
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.820
the link pack area or the link list concatenation.

29
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.820
This is done to reduce IO

30
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.820
and improve performance of frequently used programs.

31
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:54.820
A named pipe is also called a first-in, first-out or FIFO special file.

32
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.820
A FIFO is a file typically used to send data from one process to another

33
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.820
so that the receiving process reads the data in a first-in, first-out order.

34
00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:11.820
Named pipes are created by the mkFIFO function or by JCL.

35
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.820
This results in a directory entry for the FIFO being created in the file system.

36
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.820
Named pipes have to be opened and closed by all processes that are using them.

37
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.820
Named pipes can be used between independent processes

38
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.820
or by parent and by child processes.

39
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:38.820
A character special file is located in the slash dev directory in the root file system.

40
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.820
It's a file that defines a number of things.

41
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:49.820
There's a system console file that's slash dev slash console or slash dev slash oper log.

42
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:55.820
That's used to direct data to the system console or the operator log facility of zos.

43
00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:01.820
A null file or slash dev slash null is equivalent to a zos dummy file.

44
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:05.820
Slash dev slash zero is a character special device file.

45
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:11.820
Data written to this file is discarded and the data read from this file is in the form of binary zeros.

46
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.820
There's slash dev slash random which is a device file that produces random bytes.

47
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:22.820
A file descriptor file for example slash dev slash fdn used to create a duplicate of an open descriptor.

48
00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:30.820
A terminal file slash dev slash ptypnnn and slash dev slash ttypnnn

49
00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:36.820
where nnnn is a number used to represent a hardware device such as a terminal or a workstation.

50
00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:43.820
These files are built in pairs and the system selects a pair of these files to represent a connection between a user and the system.

51
00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:51.820
And finally the terminal file slash dev slash tty which is the default controlling terminal for a process.

52
00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.820
A symbolic link is a file that contains the path name for another file.

53
00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.820
In essence a reference to another file.

54
00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.820
It's somewhat comparable to a zos alias.

55
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.820
A symbolic link can be created for a file or a directory.

56
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:11.820
A symbolic link can refer to files in another file system and the symbolic link is created by the simlink function.

57
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:18.820
A symbolic link can be used when a file is moved to a new directory and you want users to access the file with the old name.

58
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:24.820
Also it can be used to create an alias short path name for a file that has a long path name.

59
00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:31.820
A hard link is a bit different from a symbolic link in that it's a new name for an existing file.

60
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:36.820
A hard link is a new entry in a directory that has the same inode as the original data.

61
00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.820
A hard link can only point to a file in the same file system.

62
00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:45.820
It can't point to a directory or across file systems and it's created with a link function.

63
00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.820
An external link is a variation of a symbolic link.

64
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:53.820
An external link is a type of symbolic link which points to a zos dataset.

65
00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:02.820
It can only be used by the network file system, the exec family of functions and to point to dynamic link libraries located in zos datasets.

66
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.820
The minus e option on the ln command creates an external link.

67
00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.820
They can also be created through the i shell.

68
00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.820
A shell user can browse data in a zos dataset through an external link.

69
00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:19.820
They can only create, delete or look at the external link's attributes.

70
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.820
We've mentioned file name, symbolic link, hard link and file descriptors which are all used for referencing a file.

71
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.820
So let's look at an overview of these file identifiers.

72
00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:34.820
A file can be identified by of course file and path name.

73
00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:39.820
Each file has a unique identification number called an inode number as well.

74
00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:45.820
The inode number refers to a physical file on DASD where the data is stored in a particular location.

75
00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:50.820
The inode number is kept in the directory together with the file name.

76
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:55.820
A symbolic link is a separate physical file with a pointer to another physical file.

77
00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:01.820
The two files have different inode numbers and they can reside in different file systems.

78
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:10.820
A hard link is a new name for an existing file and a hard link is a new entry in a directory with the same inode number as the existing file.

79
00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:14.820
Hard links can't point to data across file systems.

80
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.820
And finally, a file descriptor is a number that represents an open file.

81
00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.820
It's used to access files in C language programs.

82
00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:26.820
When a program opens a file, the system returns a file descriptor for it.

83
00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.820
OK, so that's file systems.

84
00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.820
Next up, HFS and CFS datasets.
